# {{ module_name }}

{{ description }}

A self-contained Django app (`{{ package_name }}`) that any project can install, made
for projects made from the django-starter template (v1) and working in any Django
project. It has no project of its own: its tests run against a tiny stand-in project
in `tests/`.

**Works with:** Python 3.14+, Django 6.1 or a later 6.x (tested on 6.1),
and PostgreSQL 18 (what django-starter projects run) or SQLite. CI tests both
databases on every pull request.

## Add it to a project

In a project made from django-starter:

1. Install a released version (a tag) from GitHub:

   ```bash
   uv add git+https://github.com/{{ github_owner }}/{{ distribution_name }} --tag vX.Y.Z
   ```

   If the repository is private, the project's production image needs a login while
   it builds: see "Adding a reusable app" in the project's README (a Docker build
   secret; never a token in the URL). While developing the module next to a project,
   `uv add --editable ../{{ distribution_name }}` uses your working copy instead;
   switch back to the tagged line before deploying (a folder outside the project
   can't be built into its image).

2. Add `"{{ package_name }}"` to `INSTALLED_APPS` in `config/settings/core.py`, in the
   "your apps" part at the top.
3. If the module has URLs, include them in `config/urls.py`, e.g.
   `path("{{ package_name | replace('_', '-') }}/", include("{{ package_name }}.urls"))`.
4. If the module has settings (see [Settings](#settings)), put them in the project's
   settings topic file they belong to, read from the environment, and list each new
   variable in `.env.example`.
5. Create its tables, then run the project's tests:

   ```bash
   uv run python manage.py migrate      # production's migrate step does this by itself
   uv run pytest
   ```

## Settings

None yet. Each setting a project can change is read with the
`{{ package_name | upper }}_` prefix and a sensible default, and listed here with its
default and what it does, e.g.
`getattr(settings, "{{ package_name | upper }}_CACHE_SECONDS", 300)`.

## Upgrading

Install the new tag (the same `uv add … --tag vX.Y.Z` line), then run
`uv run python manage.py migrate`, and in production deploy as usual (the image runs
`collectstatic`; the migrate step migrates). Read `CHANGELOG.md` first: each version
says what a project must do, if anything.

## Logging

The module logs through its own logger, never `print()`, and never sets up handlers
(the project decides where logs go):

```python
import logging

logger = logging.getLogger("{{ package_name }}")
```

Never log secrets, passwords, tokens or personal data; log IDs instead.

## Rules that keep it pluggable

- Never import from a project or from another module.
- Link to users with `settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL`, never a project's `User` class.
- Keep templates in `src/{{ package_name }}/templates/{{ package_name }}/` and static
  files in `src/{{ package_name }}/static/{{ package_name }}/`, so names never clash.
- Read settings with the `{{ package_name | upper }}_` prefix and a default (see Settings).
- **Never add a `check --deploy` warning** to a project (`tests/test_host_rules.py`
  checks this).
- **Pages** extend the project's `base.html` (django-starter's has `title`, `head`,
  `header`, `content` and `footer` blocks); add stylesheets and scripts in
  `{% raw %}{% block head %}{{ block.super }}…{% endblock %}{% endraw %}`. Template tags
  that read the database go in a page that extends `base.html` (the project's layout),
  never in `base.html` itself, so the project's error pages keep working when the
  database is down.
- **No inline code**: projects enforce a strict Content Security Policy. Put CSS and
  JavaScript in static files; if inline code is unavoidable, give the tag
  `nonce="{% raw %}{{ csp_nonce }}{% endraw %}"`. Never `style="…"`, `onclick=` or
  `javascript:` links; nothing from other sites (CDNs, web fonts) or `data:` URLs
  except images, and no frames, video, audio, embedded objects or manifests (a
  django-starter project's policy blocks them all). `tests/test_host_rules.py` checks
  the module's templates (`.html`, `.htm`, `.svg`, `.xml` and Jinja files under
  `templates/` and `jinja2/`) against that policy. It can't see CSS or JavaScript
  files, what inline code loads, or HTML built in Python (`format_html`), so keep the
  same rules there. A comment that hides code must be `{% raw %}{% comment %}{% endraw %}`
  or a one-line `{% raw %}{# #}{% endraw %}`: Django prints a `{% raw %}{# #}{% endraw %}`
  that spans lines.
- **Uploads:** see [Security](#security).
- **Slow work** (emails, file processing, calls to other services) goes to a
  background job with Django's Tasks API; **email** goes through `django.core.mail`.

## Security

Rules for the module's code. The project trusts whatever the module does, so:

- **Views:** every view checks login and permission (or ownership), so nobody can see
  or change someone else's data by changing an ID in the URL. Test it for anonymous
  users and for other users.
- **Forms and input:** validate all input with Django forms;
  `{% raw %}{% csrf_token %}{% endraw %}` in every POST form; never `csrf_exempt`.
- **Database:** the ORM or parameterized queries only, never SQL built from strings.
- **Templates:** keep auto-escaping; never `|safe` or `mark_safe` on anything a user
  typed.
- **Changes only on POST:** a view that changes anything refuses GET
  (`@require_POST`), so a link or an image on another site can't trigger it. Check
  redirect targets such as `next` with `url_has_allowed_host_and_scheme` (no open
  redirects).
- **Logins:** never check passwords or tokens yourself; use
  `django.contrib.auth.authenticate(request, ...)`, so the project's login rate
  limiting (django-axes) counts every attempt.
- **Uploads:** check type and size, never trust the file's name, store under a
  `{{ package_name }}/` folder with a random part per file, and refuse names with `..`
  (path traversal). In django-starter projects the default storage is **private**:
  its files are served only through the project's own view, to whoever the project's
  `can_download()` allows (by default, active staff). So a module that keeps private
  files either says in this README what projects should allow in `can_download()`,
  or serves them through its own view that checks permission. Files anyone may see
  (pictures on public pages) go in the project's "public" storage, chosen without
  importing project code (a function keeps the module's migrations the same in every
  project):

  ```python
  from django.conf import settings
  from django.core.files.storage import default_storage, storages


  def public_storage():
      # The project's public storage (django-starter projects have one), else the default.
      return storages["public"] if "public" in settings.STORAGES else default_storage
  ```

  and `models.ImageField(storage=public_storage, ...)`.
- **Background jobs:** pass IDs, never secrets, tokens or personal data, as arguments:
  they're stored in the database and shown in the admin.
- **Secrets:** never in code. A secret the module needs comes from a project setting
  (read from the environment there); fail with a clear error if it's missing.
- **Calls to other services:** HTTPS only, certificate checks on, and a timeout.
- **Logs:** see [Logging](#logging).

## Database

- **Supported:** PostgreSQL 18 and SQLite (both tested on every pull request).
- **Every migration keeps working with the previous release's code.** Projects
  migrate while the old code still runs, and a rollback doesn't undo migrations: add
  things in one release, remove them in a later one, and never rename in one step.
- **Nothing that needs a superuser** (e.g. `CREATE EXTENSION` of an untrusted
  extension): django-starter projects migrate with a login that isn't one. If the
  module needs such an extension, say so here and in the CHANGELOG, so the project
  adds it first.
- **Never edit or delete a released migration**; add a new one. Data migrations use
  `apps.get_model` and have a reverse (or `migrations.RunPython.noop`).
- **Say in the CHANGELOG** when a migration deletes data or is slow on big tables.

Create migrations after changing models (`tests/test_app.py` fails until you do):

```bash
uv run django-admin makemigrations {{ package_name }} --settings=tests.settings --pythonpath=.
```

## Develop it

Once, right after making the module:

```bash
git init                   # a new module isn't a git repo yet
uv lock                    # pins every package version in uv.lock: commit it (CI needs it)
uv sync                    # installs Python, Django and the test tools
uv run pre-commit install  # turns on the checks that run on every commit
git add -A && git commit -m "Start from django-module-starter"
```

Then create the repository on GitHub under `{{ github_owner }}`, push, and turn on
"Dependabot alerts" and "Dependabot security updates" in its Settings > Code security
(for a public repository, also secret scanning with push protection, and code
scanning).

Every day:

```bash
uv run pytest              # runs the tests on SQLite, no setup needed
uv run pytest --cov        # the same, with coverage (fails below 80%)
uv run ruff check .        # finds problems
uv run ruff format .       # formats the code
```

Before every pull request, check the dependencies for known vulnerabilities:

```bash
(f=$(mktemp) && trap 'rm -f "$f"' EXIT && uv export --locked --format requirements-txt --no-emit-project --quiet -o "$f" && uv run pip-audit --disable-pip --require-hashes -r "$f")
```

**On GitHub,** every pull request runs `.github/workflows/ci.yml`: ruff, the tests on
SQLite and on PostgreSQL 18 with coverage, pip-audit, a scan of the whole history for
secrets (gitleaks), and a check that the built package holds only the module.
Dependabot (`.github/dependabot.yml`) opens pull requests for updates.

The commit check only scans what's being committed. To scan every file and the whole
history for secrets (needs Docker; the every-file scan also reads files git ignores,
like a local `.env`, so a finding there may never have been committed):

```bash
docker run --rm -v "$PWD:/repo:ro" ghcr.io/gitleaks/gitleaks:v8.30.1@sha256:c00b6bd0aeb3071cbcb79009cb16a60dd9e0a7c60e2be9ab65d25e6bc8abbb7f dir /repo --redact   # every file
docker run --rm -v "$PWD:/repo:ro" ghcr.io/gitleaks/gitleaks:v8.30.1@sha256:c00b6bd0aeb3071cbcb79009cb16a60dd9e0a7c60e2be9ab65d25e6bc8abbb7f git /repo --redact   # the whole history
```

**Updated by hand** (Dependabot doesn't touch these), keeping each pair in step:

- **uv:** `UV_VERSION` in `.github/workflows/ci.yml` and the `uv_build` range in
  `pyproject.toml` (`>=0.12,<0.13` goes with uv 0.12).
- **gitleaks:** `rev` in `.pre-commit-config.yaml` (`uv run pre-commit autoupdate --freeze`)
  and `GITLEAKS_IMAGE` in `.github/workflows/ci.yml`, plus the two commands above.

To run the tests on Postgres here too (CI always does), with a Postgres that has that
login and database running:

```bash
DATABASE_URL=postgres://app:app@localhost:5432/module_tests uv run pytest
```

To check what the package will ship (CI does this too): `uv build`, then
`unzip -l dist/*.whl` should list only `{{ package_name }}/` and its metadata.

## Release a version

Nothing is pushed straight to `main`:

1. Open an issue for the release and a branch for it. Change `version` in
   `pyproject.toml`, run `uv lock` (CI refuses a lock file that doesn't match), and add
   the version at the top of `CHANGELOG.md`: what changed, and what a project must do
   when it upgrades (newest version first). Commit `pyproject.toml`, `uv.lock` and
   `CHANGELOG.md`.
2. Try the branch in a project made from django-starter: install your working copy
   (`uv add --editable ../{{ distribution_name }}`, as in
   [Add it to a project](#add-it-to-a-project)), run the project's tests (they include
   `check --deploy` with production settings), and try it in the project's local
   production run (its README, "Production"). Switch the project back to the tag
   afterwards.
3. Open a pull request; CI must pass (both databases, the package check). Merge it.
4. Tag the merged `main` and push only the tag:

   ```bash
   git switch main && git pull
   git tag vX.Y.Z
   git push origin vX.Y.Z
   ```

5. In each project, install the new tag when you want it (see [Upgrading](#upgrading)).

## Updating from the starter

When django-module-starter improves, commit everything first, then:

```bash
copier update
```

Copier re-applies the starter's changes and asks about any conflicts (keep your own
changes, and the newer of two pinned versions). To change the GitHub owner (or the
license or copyright year) the module was made with (this also brings in the
starter's newest release, as `copier update` always does):

```bash
copier update --defaults --data github_owner=NEW-OWNER
```

If the repository itself moved, also point git at it:
`git remote set-url origin https://github.com/NEW-OWNER/{{ distribution_name }}.git`.

## Files

| Path | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| `src/{{ package_name }}/` | The Django app that projects install |
| `src/{{ package_name }}/apps.py` | The app's name, label and admin title |
| `src/{{ package_name }}/models.py` | Database models (empty to start) |
| `src/{{ package_name }}/migrations/` | Database migrations (see Database) |
| `tests/settings.py` | A tiny stand-in project for the tests |
| `tests/urls.py` | The stand-in project's URLs; add the module's here once it has some |
| `tests/test_app.py` | Checks the app loads from its own package and its migrations are complete |
| `tests/test_host_rules.py` | Checks the module adds no `check --deploy` warning to a project, and its templates have nothing a strict Content Security Policy blocks |
| `LICENSE` | The module's license ({{ license }}) |
| `pyproject.toml` | Package name, version, Django versions supported, tool settings |
| `CHANGELOG.md` | What changed in each version, and what projects must do |
| `uv.lock` | The exact version of every package, so tests and CI install the same thing (commit it) |
| `.pre-commit-config.yaml` | The checks on every commit: gitleaks (secrets) and ruff |
| `.github/workflows/ci.yml` | The checks on every pull request (see Develop it) |
| `.github/dependabot.yml` | Weekly pull requests for package and CI updates |
| `.gitignore` | Keeps secrets, keys, local databases and test output out of git |
| `.python-version` | The Python version uv installs |
| `.copier-answers.yml` | Lets `copier update` find the template; don't edit |

